In this lesson we'll look at dot plots, a really simple way to picture a small data set. The idea is to draw a horizontal number line from the smallest to the largest value, then for every piece of data, place a single dot above that number. If a value shows up more than once, you just stack the dots into a little column, one on top of the other. So a tall column means that number appears a lot, and a lonely dot means it only shows up once. Now, what can you actually read off a dot plot? You can look at the shape, so is it symmetric, or does it have a longer tail trailing off to one side? You can spot the centre, which is roughly where most of the dots are bunching up. You can judge the spread by how far apart the smallest and largest dots sit. And importantly, you can pick out outliers, values that sit all by themselves with no neighbours nearby. The big advantage is that nothing is hidden. Every single observation is right there as a dot, so nothing gets summarised away. It's quick to draw, and it works beautifully for small data sets, maybe up to thirty values. Now let's see it in action.